In the early 1970s, programmers wrote lexers by hand—hundreds of repetitive, error-prone loops testing one character after another. Then, in 1975 at Bell Labs, Mike Lesk and summer intern Eric Schmidt realized that nearly every token category in a programming language is simply a regular expression. Why hand-code a scanner when a little language could *generate* it?

Their tool was **Lex**, later joined by Vern Paxson's faster open-source successor **Flex** (*Fast Lexical Analyzer*). A developer writes a short specification pairing each regex pattern with a tiny code action to run when it matches:

```text
"while"                { return WHILE; }
[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]* { return IDENT; }
[0-9]+                 { return NUMBER; }
```

Feed that specification into Lex or Flex (where `IDENT` labels an *identifier*, such as a variable name), and the generator outputs ready-to-compile code in the C programming language for a complete scanner. Adding a new keyword or number format becomes a one-line edit instead of an afternoon of tricky manual debugging.
